<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<!--
    What the app claims for Debug and Profile builds.

    A provisioning profile GRANTS rights; the app has to CLAIM them, and until
    2026-08-31 this file did not exist at all. The App Store profile carried
    `aps-environment: production` and the binary asked for nothing, so
    `registerForRemoteNotifications` was refused with "no valid aps-environment"
    - the same message a build signed by the wrong team gets, which is why the
    team looked like the whole problem for a week. Both had to be fixed, and
    the profile was only the half that was visible.

    `development` here and `production` in RunnerRelease.entitlements, because
    the two are different APNs environments with different device tokens and
    each host refuses the other's. A Debug build talks to sandbox; anything
    exported for TestFlight or the App Store talks to production. Claiming
    `production` in a Debug build fails to install rather than failing to push,
    since the development profile does not grant it.
-->
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
	<key>aps-environment</key>
	<string>development</string>
</dict>
</plist>
